---
// Lesson 12.1a — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Kickoff & Pitch
// rpg jam: post-exam jam. Grid-based design; symmetry as generator.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>12.1a — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Kickoff &amp; Pitch</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The exam is behind you. The games are not. Third jam, and the
    constraint is structural this time: a GRID with SYMMETRY — the
    design language of Tetris, Picross, chess, Snake, Minesweeper.
    When the grid is the rule, every design decision multiplies.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Kickoff</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What the constraint means:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Grid:</strong> movement, placement, or both
            snap to cells — think in indices, not coordinates
            (your <a href="7.1a.html">7.1a</a> tilemap was a grid
            you PAINTED; this one you PROGRAM)</li>
        <li><strong>Symmetry:</strong> the game state, the board,
            or the CONTROLS mirror somehow — vertical (chess:
            both sides same), rotational (Tetris: each piece is
            its own mirror), or player-vs-player (both play
            identical rules)</li>
        <li>Either half may dominate: a grid game with light
            symmetry, or a symmetric duel on a grid</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>You have already done grid math without noticing:</strong>
    your hand of cards rendered by index
    (<a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a> — position = index × spacing).
    A grid is that, in two axes: <code>x = column * cellSize</code>.
    The symmetry half? Your turret personalities
    (<a href="3.4a.html">3.4a</a>) were mirrored difficulty —
    same idea, one axis up.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Symmetry is a GENERATOR: define one half, mirror it,
            get the second half free. Where in code does that
            save work (spawn positions? level halves? an AI
            that plays YOUR OWN rules — like your card AI
            (<a href="10.2d.html">10.2d</a>) but as player
            two)? Which symmetry would make your game's
            content FREE?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pitch &amp; Vote</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Pitch card (10 min):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>One sentence: "a grid game where ___ is symmetric
            because ___"</li>
        <li>The core loop: one move, its consequence, why it
            repeats</li>
        <li>The symmetry's JOB: fairness (duel)? difficulty
            (mirror match)? beauty (pattern play)? — name
            it</li>
        <li>Reused-parts list (<a href="10.1a.html">10.1a</a>
            rules: honest inventory)</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>30-second pitches → votes → teams of 1–3 (same
    contracts as every jam).</strong> This is the last new-team
    formation of the year: choose for who you work WELL with,
    not who you sit near.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Pitch-time scope checks</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A pitch with BOTH real-time action AND grid
                logic. Which half dies first under deadline —
                and what does "turn-based or nothing" buy the
                last jam of the year?</li>
            <li>Symmetry of RULES but not of BOARD (chess has
                both). Which one can you BUILD in two classes —
                and which is just an if-statement?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Tonight</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Jam README with the pitch card + the INPUT table
                (<a href="10.1a.html">10.1a</a> format). What
                does "press W" MEAN on a grid (column, row,
                +/−1) — write the mapping now.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name three real grid games and their symmetry type</li>
            <li>Pitch a grid+symmetry loop in one sentence</li>
            <li>Define the input → grid mapping in your README</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — a grid is a list of lists</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-12/index.html">Unit 12: Season 2</a> — the last jams</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="12.1b.html">12.1b — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 1</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="12.1a" title="12.1a Grid/Symmetry Jam: Kickoff &amp; Pitch — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
